rgit — remaining git commands that fit (round 2)
=================================================
Already implemented (113 + 3 synonyms, checked against src/cli.rs on 2026-11-16): add am annotate apply archive banana-split bisect blame branch bundle cat-file check-ignore check-mailmap
check-ref-format checkout checkout-index cherry cherry-pick clean clone column commit commit-graph commit-tree config
count-objects describe diff diff-files diff-index diff-tree fast-export fast-import fetch fmt-merge-msg for-each-ref
for-each-repo format-patch fsck gc get-tar-commit-id grep hash-object help hook index-pack init interpret-trailers
last-modified log ls-files ls-tree mailinfo mailsplit maintenance merge merge-base merge-file merge-tree mktag mktree
multi-pack-index mv name-rev notes pack-objects pack-refs patch-id prune prune-packed pull push range-diff read-tree
rebase reflog refs remote repack replace replay repo request-pull rerere reset restore rev-list rev-parse revert rm
shortlog show show-branch show-index show-ref sparse-checkout stash status stripspace submodule switch symbolic-ref
tag unpack-file unpack-objects update-index update-ref var verify-pack version whatchanged worktree write-tree
(synonyms: stage = add, init-db = init, fsck-objects = fsck)

Source: `git help -a` (2.43) + release notes up to git 2.52 (Nov 2025).
Legend: ⭐ = recommended next   [S]/[M]/[L] = size   "reuses:" = existing rgit code it builds on


LEFT TO DO AT A GLANCE (0 commands; details in the sections below)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Quick wins (0):       all done (refs, name-rev, var, stripspace, column, version, help, stage, init-db, fsck-objects)
2. Message/patch (0):    all done (interpret-trailers, patch-id, mailsplit, mailinfo, check-mailmap, fmt-merge-msg, request-pull)
3. Index/tree (0):      all done (read-tree, update-index, checkout-index, mktree, mktag, diff-files, diff-index,
                         diff-tree, merge-file, unpack-file, get-tar-commit-id)
4. History (0):          all done (range-diff, show-branch, last-modified, repo, notes, replace, whatchanged, annotate)
5. Workflow (0):         all done (rerere, hook, replay, for-each-repo, maintenance, sparse-checkout, submodule)
6. Packfiles (0):        all done (pack-objects, unpack-objects, index-pack, verify-pack, show-index, repack,
                         prune-packed, commit-graph, multi-pack-index, fast-export, fast-import)
Skipped on purpose:      everything under "Probably not worth it" at the bottom.


1. Quick ref & plumbing wins  ✅ ALL DONE
------------------------------------------
refs (list / exists / verify / optimize; `migrate` refused: no reftable backend), name-rev, var, stripspace, column,
version, help, and the synonyms stage / init-db / fsck-objects.
Notes: `refs` could not be compared with a real git (needs 2.46+; only 2.43 was available). `var` reports rgit's
own settings (RGIT_EDITOR, ~/.rgitconfig, UTC timestamps), under git's variable names.


2. Message, patch & identity tools  ✅ ALL DONE
----------------------------------------------
interpret-trailers, patch-id, mailsplit, mailinfo, check-mailmap, fmt-merge-msg, request-pull.
Follow-ups they make possible: `am` could call the new mailsplit/mailinfo helpers; for-each-ref `:mailmap`,
shortlog, blame and log `%aN/%aE` could honour .mailmap through helpers/mailmap.rs; mailinfo only converts
UTF-8, US-ASCII, ISO-8859-1/15 and Windows-1252 (the `encoding_rs` crate would cover the rest).


3. Index / tree plumbing  ✅ ALL DONE
-------------------------------------
read-tree, update-index, checkout-index, mktree, mktag, diff-files, diff-index, diff-tree, merge-file, unpack-file,
get-tar-commit-id. `archive` now also writes git's pax global header (commit id) into tar archives.

Limits that come from rgit's index (version 2, one entry per path, no flag bits) — the same prerequisite as
sparse-checkout in section 5:
  - read-tree -m with 3 trees resolves only what needs no stages (trivial cases, plus --aggressive); a path that
    git would leave unmerged (stages 1-3) makes it fail instead.
  - update-index: no --assume-unchanged / --skip-worktree / --unresolve, no stage numbers in --index-info.
  - checkout-index --stage=1..3 finds nothing; --stage=all prints nothing.
Other differences from git:
  - A file whose cached stat data is stale but whose content is unchanged counts as clean (git needs
    `update-index --refresh` first). Affects diff-files, diff-index, checkout-index, read-tree -m.
  - diff-tree / diff-index / diff-files: no combined diffs (-c, --cc) and no -B (rename detection: done); with
    --pretty/--format, %h stays abbreviated (git's diff-tree prints it in full).
  - Symlinks are handled like regular files, as everywhere in rgit.


4. History & inspection  ✅ ALL DONE
-----------------------------------
range-diff, show-branch, last-modified, repo, notes, replace, whatchanged, annotate.
Known gaps: `notes merge` conflicts need -s ours|theirs|union|cat_sort_uniq (no NOTES_MERGE_WORKTREE workflow);
`show-branch --reflog` and `replace --convert-graft-file` are missing; last-modified and repo follow git 2.52's
documentation but could not be compared against a real 2.52.
Fixed afterwards (were spotted while testing): revision parsing now accepts `heads/<name>` / `tags/<name>` /
`remotes/<name>`, `@`, and mixed suffix chains like `<rev>~N^0`; a tag wins over a branch of the same name (with
git's "refname is ambiguous" warning); `log`/`show` no longer print a blank line for an empty commit message.


5. Workflow features  ✅ ALL DONE
---------------------------------
hook (run), for-each-repo, maintenance (run/register/unregister), replay, rerere, sparse-checkout, submodule.
Follow-ups: fire pre-commit / commit-msg / post-commit / pre-push from rgit's own commands through helpers/hook.rs;
call rerere from merge / rebase / cherry-pick / commit when rerere.enabled is set.

Index groundwork done for these two: index version 3 is read and written, entries carry the skip-worktree bit
(`update-index --[no-]skip-worktree` works now), and gitlink entries (mode 160000) are understood by checkout,
reset, status, add, diff, archive, gc and local fetch/push. Still missing in the index: conflict stages and
the assume-unchanged bit.

sparse-checkout: init / set / add / list / reapply / disable / check-rules, cone and non-cone patterns, same files
and config as git.
  - Commands that compare the working tree (status, diff, commit -a, stash, switch, diff-files, diff-index,
    ls-files, checkout-index, update-index --refresh) treat left-out files as unchanged. Commands that rewrite
    the working tree (checkout, switch, reset, merge, pull, rebase, cherry-pick, revert, stash, restore, ...)
    write everything and the patterns are applied again afterwards, so files outside the selection appear and
    disappear during the command; a command that stops half-way (a conflict) leaves them until the next one.
  - No sparse index (`--sparse-index`), no `clean` subcommand (git 2.52), no escaping of special characters in
    cone directory names.

submodule: add / status / init / update / deinit / foreach / sync / set-url / set-branch.
  - The submodule's repository lives in `<path>/.git` (not `.git/modules/<name>`; git accepts both). `deinit`
    parks it under `.git/modules/<name>` and `update` restores it.
  - No `summary`, `absorbgitdirs`, `update --remote/--merge/--rebase`, `clone --recurse-submodules`; `merge`
    takes a changed gitlink only when one side changed it; `status` shows `(new commits)` but not
    modified/untracked content inside a submodule; `submodule status` names commits by exact tag or branch
    only (git also prints forms like `v1~1`).
  - Tags of a submodule are only as available as rgit's clone/fetch makes them.


6. Packfiles  ✅ ALL DONE
-------------------------
pack-objects, unpack-objects, index-pack, verify-pack, show-index, repack, prune-packed, commit-graph,
multi-pack-index, fast-export, fast-import.
Object reads now fall back to packfiles (idx v1/v2, OFS/REF deltas), so every command works on packed
repositories; fsck, short hashes, gc/prune and local fetch/push/clone (incl. the remote's packed-refs) know packs.

Left out / different from git:
  - commit-graph and multi-pack-index files are written in git's format (byte-identical in tests) but rgit does
    not read them itself yet: walks still open commit objects, lookups still go through each pack's .idx.
    No split graphs, no Bloom filters (--changed-paths), no bitmaps, no `multi-pack-index repack`.
  - pack-objects / repack: own delta heuristic (16-byte block matching, window 10, depth 50), so packs are valid
    but not as small as git's; no thin packs on output, no .rev / .bitmap files, no --max-pack-size.
  - gc still only prunes and expires reflogs; it does not call repack.
  - fast-import writes loose objects (not a pack), prints statistics only with --stats, and has no
    cat-blob / ls / --cat-blob-fd; notes (`N`) are stored without fan-out.
  - fast-export: no -M/-C, --anonymize, --refspec, --reencode or path limiting; a few corner cases with
    negative revisions (`--all --not x`, `A...B`) name or reset refs differently from git.


Probably not worth it (and why)
-------------------------------
- GUI / web: gui, citool, gitk, gitweb, instaweb.
- Foreign VCS & email transport: svn, p4, cvs*, archimport, quiltimport, send-email, imap-send.
- Network servers & credentials: daemon, http-backend, update-server-info, send-pack/fetch-pack internals,
  receive-pack, upload-pack, upload-archive, http-fetch, http-push, remote-* transport helpers, shell,
  credential*, ls-remote (only if rgit's fetch grows a generic smart-HTTP client beyond the GitHub API).
- GPG/SSH signing: verify-commit, verify-tag (needs gpg/ssh-keygen).
- External tool launchers: difftool, mergetool.
- Deprecated / niche: filter-branch (use fast-export instead), pack-redundant, merge-index/merge-one-file,
  merge-octopus/-ours/-recursive/-resolve/-subtree (strategy backends), subtree (contrib), pickaxe (internal
  blame alias), diff-pairs (2.50, server-side diff plumbing), sh-setup/sh-i18n, scalar, backfill (partial clone), refs migrate (reftable), bugreport/diagnose.
- check-attr: only worth it together with full .gitattributes support (eol/text/diff drivers).


Suggested order
---------------
refs → name-rev → merge-file → diff-tree/diff-index/
diff-files → read-tree/update-index → index flags (skip-worktree, stages) → sparse-checkout → pack reading + pack-objects.


=====================================================================================================
ROUND 3 — "rgit stops copying git and starts being rgit"            (written 2026-11-16)
=====================================================================================================
Every git command worth porting is ported. From here the value is NOT in more clones of git commands,
it is in (A) closing the fidelity gaps that make the existing commands feel incomplete, (B) new commands
git doesn't have, and (C) a "verbose / user-friendly" identity that makes rgit the git you can actually
understand. Same legend as above: ⭐ = recommended next, [S]/[M]/[L] = size, "reuses:" = existing code.


A. FIDELITY GAPS — things users will hit within the first hour
--------------------------------------------------------------
✅ log --graph              DONE 2026-11-17 (helpers/graph.rs, port of graph.c; identical to git 2.43 on 2080 fuzzed cases).
✅ log --follow             DONE 2026-11-17 (helpers/follow.rs; git's similarity scoring; identical on ~4150 fuzzed cases).
                              Known gaps: show/whatchanged reject --graph/--follow; non-UTF-8 text lines in -p are shown lossily
                              (pre-existing in the line differ, plain log -p too); plain log -p/--stat still has no rename detection.
✅ add -p / restore -p / reset -p / checkout -p / stash -p   DONE 2026-11-17 (helpers/add_patch.rs + commands/patch.rs;
                              port of add-patch.c, all 9 git modes; matches git 2.43 on ~98% of 1980 fuzzed sessions).
                              Known differences: no `git apply` whitespace warnings; editing a split hunk can differ
                              from git's merge_hunks quirks; git 2.43 bugs not copied (editing the mode-change hunk
                              crashes git; reset -p rejects both halves of a split hunk at line 1).
✅ rename detection (-M, -C)  DONE 2026-11-17 (helpers/rename.rs, port of diffcore-rename; diff (rebuilt on the raw
                              engine), log/show/whatchanged (+ --summary), status, diff-tree/-index/-files, fast-export;
                              matches git 2.43 on ~5,600 fuzzed runs; the only misses are 2 hunk-placement cases of
                              rgit's line differ on plain modifications). Not yet: blame -M/-C, format-patch renames,
                              -B (break rewrites), status --short.
✅ Index conflict stages 1-3  DONE 2026-11-18 (index.rs stage bits; helpers/merge.rs merge_into_worktree +
                              write_conflict_stages; helpers/combine_diff.rs, port of combine-diff.c). merge, cherry-pick,
                              revert and rebase now share merge's line-level ort merge and write stages 1/2/3 byte-identical
                              to git; status (Unmerged paths + state line), ls-files -s/-u/-t, diff/diff-files/diff-index
                              (U, * Unmerged path, Unmerged in --stat, -1/-2/-3, diff --cc), add/rm resolve, commit/-a,
                              write-tree, merge/cherry-pick/revert/rebase/stash/switch/checkout/reset --soft refuse like git,
                              restore/checkout --ours/--theirs/-m/--conflict, reset clears. Fixed on the way: mode-only
                              changes were dropped by merge and switch; status/reset ignored mode changes; status sections
                              now in path order. Not yet: checkout-index --stage on stages, update-index --unresolve /
                              --index-info stages, read-tree -m writing stages, stash apply conflicts, rerere from index
                              stages, rebase status header ("interactive rebase in progress").
✅ rebase --onto            DONE 2026-11-18 (commands/rebase.rs; select_commits_to_rebase / can_fast_forward /
                              commit_is_empty in helpers/rebase.rs). --onto <newbase>, --keep-base, <branch>,
                              --[no-]reapply-cherry-picks, upstream from branch config; git's commit selection
                              (upstream...HEAD right side, no merges, topo order, already-applied commits skipped with
                              git's warning), "Current branch <b> is up to date.", Rebasing (i/n) progress, git's
                              success line on stderr, fast-forwarded picks, ORIG_HEAD, error messages. Matches git 2.43
                              on all ~2,600 fuzzed runs (100 repos × 26 scenarios: onto/keep-base/branch/detached/errors/
                              tracking/applied commits/conflict+continue/skip/abort).
                              Fixed on the way: rebased commits lost their original author and date (rgit stamped your
                              identity and "now"); commits that start empty were dropped (git keeps them); rgit-only
                              "Rebasing N commit(s)", "[detached x]", "Skipped ..." and "Rebase aborted" lines removed.
                              Not yet: --autosquash, --rebase-merges, --exec, --root, git's .git/rebase-merge state
                              layout (rgit keeps its own REBASE_* files), status "rebase in progress" lines.
   rebase --autosquash / --rebase-merges / --exec [M]
   commit --fixup=<c> / --squash=<c>  (pairs with rebase --autosquash) [S]
✅ merge flags               DONE 2026-11-18 (commands/merge.rs; merge_trees_favoring in helpers/merge_tree.rs;
                              abort_merge_to_head / clear_merge_state in helpers/merge.rs). --ff/--no-ff/--ff-only,
                              --squash (SQUASH_MSG; commit reads SQUASH_MSG + MERGE_MSG like git), --[no-]commit, -m/-F,
                              -s ort|recursive|ours, -X ours|theirs, -n/-q, --allow-unrelated-histories, --abort,
                              --continue, --quit, detached HEAD; diffstat + summary after merges and fast-forwards
                              (was missing); " into <branch>" per merge.suppressDest; ORIG_HEAD/MERGE_MODE/AUTO_MERGE.
                              Matches git 2.43 on ~4,100 fuzzed runs (100 repos × 41 scenarios); the only misses: commit
                              hashes (rgit ignores GIT_AUTHOR_DATE / GIT_COMMITTER_DATE) and merge --abort on a
                              file/directory conflict, where git itself fails ("not uptodate") and rgit aborts cleanly.
                              Fixed on the way: commit --no-edit stripped "#" lines (git keeps them without an editor);
                              reset reported D instead of T for a directory where a file was.
                              Not yet: octopus, -s resolve/subtree, -X patience/ignore-space-*/renames, --autostash,
                              --log, -e (editor for the merge message), hooks.
✅ cherry-pick -x / ranges    DONE 2026-11-18 (commands/cherry_pick.rs rewritten as a port of git's sequencer for
                              picks; helpers/sequencer.rs for .git/sequencer state and the -x / -s trailers). Commit
                              lists and ranges (A..B, ^X), -x, -s, -n, -m <n>, --ff, --allow-empty,
                              --allow-empty-message, --keep-redundant-commits, --continue, --skip, --abort, --quit;
                              original author kept, git's summary, MERGE_MSG/CHERRY_PICK_HEAD/AUTO_MERGE and
                              sequencer todo/head/abort-safety/opts byte-identical to git. Matches git 2.43 on
                              ~5,100 fuzzed runs (100 repos × 51 scenarios); the only misses: --skip/--abort after a
                              file/directory conflict, where git itself fails ("not uptodate") and rgit resets
                              cleanly. Fixed on the way: rgit ignored GIT_AUTHOR_* / GIT_COMMITTER_* (so commit hashes
                              never matched git's in scripts); commit summary had no rename detection and showed
                              " Date:" by timestamp instead of git's rule; commit --cleanup=strip; reset never wrote
                              ORIG_HEAD; add/add with only a 100644/100755 clash printed a made-up "CONFLICT (mode)"
                              instead of ort's "CONFLICT (add/add)"; status trailers now use git's wording.
                              Not yet: git's status advice hints in status output (e.g. "(use \"git cherry-pick
                              --skip\" ...)"), -e, --strategy/-X, rerere, revert ranges (revert still single-commit).
