B. NEW COMMANDS git doesn't have  (the "rgit-only" shelf, in the spirit of banana-split)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Everyday workflow
⭐ rgit undo [S-M]              Undo the last rgit operation (commit, merge, reset --hard, rebase...). Needs an operation
                               log (see C.6). Shows what it is about to undo; `rgit undo --list`, `rgit redo`.
⭐ rgit oops [S]                "I just did something wrong": looks at the reflog + op log and offers the 3 most likely
                               fixes (undo last commit keeping changes, move commit to another branch, restore deleted branch).
⭐ rgit save / rgit wip [S]     One-word stash+commit: `rgit save "halfway through parser"` makes a WIP commit on a hidden
                               ref (refs/rgit/wip/<branch>), restores a clean tree. `rgit save --list / --restore`.
   rgit sync [M]                fetch + rebase-or-merge current branch on its upstream + (optionally) push, one command,
                               with a plan printed first. `--dry-run` shows the plan only.
   rgit start <name> / rgit finish [S]   Create a topic branch from the default branch / merge it back and delete it.
   rgit amend [S]               Fold staged changes into HEAD without opening the editor (commit --amend --no-edit, shorter).
⭐ rgit fixup [M]               Pick a recent commit (interactive list or `rgit fixup <commit>`), commit staged changes as a
                               fixup of it and autosquash immediately. (git needs 3 commands for this.)
   rgit absorb [L]              Like git-absorb: split staged hunks and attach each to the commit that last touched those lines
                               (blame + fixup). reuses: blame.rs, rebase.rs.
   rgit split <commit> [M]      Interactive split of ONE commit into several by hunk (banana-split is by path/dir; this is by hunk).
   rgit squash-range <a>..<b> [S]   Squash a range into one commit without writing a todo file.
   rgit move-commit <commit> <branch> [M]   Cherry-pick a commit onto another branch and drop it from the current one.
   rgit uncommit [S]            `reset --soft HEAD~1` with a proper explanation of where the changes went.
   rgit branch-clean [S]        List + delete merged / gone-upstream / stale local branches (with a preview and confirmation).
   rgit rename-branch [S]       Rename local branch + upstream tracking + remote branch together.
   rgit ignore <pattern> [S]    Append to .gitignore (dedup, sorted, optional --global) and untrack already-tracked matches.
   rgit default-branch [S]      Show / change the default branch name everywhere it is referenced.

Understanding a repository
⭐ rgit whereami [S]            One screen: branch, upstream, ahead/behind, last commit, in-progress operation (merge? rebase?
                               bisect?), stash count, and the NEXT likely command. (Friendlier than `status` + `branch -vv` + `log -1`.)
⭐ rgit story <file|path> [M]   Human-readable history of a file: who created it, big rewrites, renames, who touches it most,
                               when it last changed. reuses: log, blame, last-modified, shortlog.
   rgit hotspots [M]            Files that change most / are touched by most authors / are touched together (coupling).
   rgit who <path|symbol> [S]   "Who knows this code?" ranked by surviving blame lines + recent commits.
   rgit stats [M]               Commits per day/week, authors, languages by lines, churn, biggest commits. Table + sparkline output.
   rgit timeline [S]            Compact day-by-day activity digest of the repo ("Mon: 4 commits by Sebi on feature/x...").
   rgit diff-summary <a> <b> [M]  Plain-English summary: "3 files added, 2 renamed, parser.rs mostly rewritten (82% changed)".
   rgit tree [S]                `ls-tree -r` rendered as a tree with sizes / last-change column.
   rgit size [S]                Repo size report: biggest blobs, biggest trees, what gc would reclaim. reuses: count-objects, pack.rs.
   rgit big-files [S]           Find large objects in history (with the commit that added them) — pre-filter-repo helper.
   rgit conflicts [S]           List unresolved files, how many conflict hunks each, which side touched what.
   rgit compare <a> <b> [S]     "Is a ahead of b / behind / diverged, by how many commits, which files differ" in one answer.
   rgit find <text> [M]         Search across history, commit messages AND current files in one command (log -S/-G + grep + --grep).

Health & safety
⭐ rgit doctor [M]              fsck + config sanity + hooks executable + stale locks (.git/*.lock) + broken worktrees + missing
                               user.name/email + huge untracked files — each finding with a fix command. reuses: fsck, refs verify, repo_info.
   rgit lock-clean [S]          Remove stale *.lock files safely (checks no rgit process owns them).
   rgit secrets [M]             Scan staged changes / history for obvious secrets (AWS keys, tokens, private keys); hook-friendly.
   rgit lint-msg [S]            Check commit messages (length, imperative mood, issue ref); usable as commit-msg hook.
   rgit verify-clean [S]        Exit 0 only if tree is clean + no untracked — for scripts/CI.
   rgit backup [S]              `bundle create --all` into ~/.rgit-backups/<repo>-<date>.bundle with retention.

Release & collaboration
   rgit changelog <a>..<b> [M]  Group commits by conventional-commit type (feat/fix/docs) into Markdown. reuses: log, shortlog.
   rgit release-notes <tag> [S] Same, between the previous tag and <tag>, with contributors list.
   rgit bump [S]                Compute next semver from conventional commits since last tag; optionally create the tag.
   rgit pr-summary [S]          Description draft for a PR: commits, files changed, diffstat. reuses: request-pull.
   rgit open [S]                Print / open the GitHub URL for a file, commit, branch or PR. reuses: helpers/github.rs.
   rgit contributors [S]        shortlog + mailmap + first/last commit date per author.

Fun / identity
   rgit banana-merge [S]        Inverse of banana-split: fold several per-path commits back into one.
   rgit banana-split --by-hunk  Extension of the existing command.
   rgit bingo / rgit tip [S]    A random "git tip of the day" taken from the glossary below (teaching feature).
   rgit zen [S]                 Prints a short project philosophy ("Explain first, act second").


C. HOW TO MAKE rgit UNIQUE: "the git that explains itself"
-----------------------------------------------------------
Thesis: git's commands are powerful but ambiguous (checkout does 3 things, reset does 3, `git branch -d` vs -D, "detached
HEAD", "fast-forward", "unmerged paths"...). Output is terse, errors are written for people who already know git.
rgit's USP: the same commands, same on-disk format (so real git can still read the repo), but every ambiguous or
destructive moment is narrated in plain language, with the exact next command to run.

C.1  Verbosity levels (global)                                               [M, do this FIRST, everything else hangs on it]
     rgit.verbosity = quiet | normal | explain | teach        (config key; env RGIT_VERBOSITY; flags -q / -v / --explain / --teach)
       quiet    git-identical output (scripts, parity tests against real git keep working).
       normal   today's behaviour + the 3-part errors from C.2.
       explain  adds one "what just happened / why" block after ambiguous commands.
       teach    explain + shows the underlying plumbing steps ("1. wrote tree 4f2a…, 2. wrote commit 9c1b…, 3. moved branch main").
     Implementation: one helper `ui::say(Level, ...)` + a `ui::explain!` macro in a new src/ui.rs; commands never println! raw prose.
     Keeps parity: tests compare `quiet` output with real git, humans get `normal`+.

C.2  Three-part errors: WHAT happened / WHY / FIX                          [M]
     Replace `fatal: pathspec 'x' did not match any file(s) known to rgit` with:
         error: nothing matches 'x'
         why:   'x' is not in the index and not in HEAD (closest: 'src/x.rs', 'x_test.rs')
         fix:   rgit status          # see what rgit knows about
                rgit add -N x        # if x is a brand-new file
     - Typo suggestions ("did you mean 'switch'?") from edit distance over command names, branch names, tags, paths.
     - One shared `fail()` (today it is duplicated: commit.rs, tag.rs ...) → `ui::fail(What, Why, Fix)`.
     - Stable error codes (E0101...) + `rgit explain-error E0101` (like rustc --explain). Fits a Rust project well.

C.3  Disambiguate the notoriously ambiguous commands                       [S each]
     checkout   Always print how the argument was interpreted:
                  "'main' is a branch -> switching branches"  /  "'main' is also a file -> restoring the FILE (use `rgit switch main` for the branch)".
                  Warn when a name matches both a branch and a path, a tag and a branch (you already warn on tag/branch ambiguity).
                  Suggest `switch` / `restore` equivalents every time, quietly teaching the modern commands.
     reset      Before acting, print the mode in words:
                  --soft:  "HEAD moves to abc123. Your index and files are untouched."
                  --mixed: "HEAD moves; staged changes become unstaged. Files untouched."
                  --hard:  "HEAD moves AND 4 modified files + 2 staged files will be overwritten. THIS LOSES WORK." + list of files.
                Plain `reset --hard` with dirty files: auto-save a safety snapshot (rgit/undo ref) and say how to get it back.
     status     Prose header: "You are on branch feature/x, 2 commits ahead of origin/feature/x (not pushed yet)."
                Group by intent: "Ready to commit (staged)", "Changed but not staged", "New files rgit isn't tracking".
                End with "Next:" line (`rgit add <file>` / `rgit commit` / `rgit push`). Mid-merge/rebase/bisect: say which and how to
                continue or abort. Detached HEAD: explain it in one sentence + how to get back (`rgit switch -`).
     merge      Announce the plan first: "fast-forward possible: main will simply move from a1 to b2" OR
                "diverged: creating a merge commit; 3 files changed on both sides". After: list conflicts with a hint per file.
     rebase     Print the plan ("replaying 4 commits of feature/x on top of main (c3d4)") and, for each todo action in -i, a one-line
                gloss (pick = keep, reword = keep but edit message, squash = meld into previous...) in the todo file comments.
                `rebase --explain` = dry run that shows what would happen to each commit.
     branch -d / -D   "Branch has 3 commits not on any other branch — they'd become unreachable (recoverable via reflog until <date>)".
     stash      "Saved 3 modified + 1 staged file as stash@{0}: <msg>. Bring them back with `rgit stash pop`."
     pull       Say which it did: fetch + merge or fetch + rebase, and why (config pull.rebase), plus what changed (n commits, m files).
     push       Explain rejections: "remote has commits you don't have (non-fast-forward). Options: 1) rgit pull 2) rgit push --force-with-lease (overwrites)".
     clean      Always preview (default -n) with sizes; `-f` prints a count of removed files.
     restore    "Discarding changes in a.rs — not recoverable." vs "Unstaging a.rs (file contents untouched)."
     commit     After commit, one line in explain mode: "Recorded 3 files (+41 −7) on main; HEAD is now 9c1b2a0. Not pushed yet."
     log        `--explain` header: "Showing commits reachable from HEAD, newest first, 20 of 143".
     tag        "Lightweight tag (just a name for a commit)" vs "Annotated tag (has author, date, message)" stated on creation.
     diff       Header says what is compared with what: "working tree vs index (unstaged changes)" / "index vs HEAD (staged)".
                (git's bare `diff` vs `diff --cached` confuses everybody.)
     config     `config --explain <key>`: where each value comes from (local / global / default) and what the key does.

C.4  `rgit explain <any rgit command line>`                                  [M-L, very distinctive]
     Narrates a command WITHOUT running it: `rgit explain reset --hard HEAD~2` →
        "Moves the current branch back 2 commits (to 7d1e…), resets the index to match, overwrites working files. Commits
         3f2a… and 8b9c… stay recoverable via reflog. 2 uncommitted files would be lost: a.rs, b.rs."
     Same info as C.3 but on demand, and usable as a safety net: `rgit --plan <command>` = explain + ask [y/N] + run.
     Universal `--dry-run` / `--plan` for every state-changing command (many already have -n; unify them).

C.5  `rgit why <thing>` / `rgit what is <thing>`                             [S-M]
     rgit why detached        → why HEAD is detached, what it means, how to fix.
     rgit why ignored <path>  → check-ignore -v in plain words (which .gitignore line, which file).
     rgit why conflict <path> → which commits on each side changed which lines.
     rgit why behind          → which commits the remote has that you don't.
     rgit what is HEAD~3 / @{u} / main...feature   → resolves any revision expression and explains the syntax used.
     rgit glossary [term]     → built-in glossary (index, staging area, HEAD, detached HEAD, fast-forward, reflog...). Terms link from explain output.

C.6  Operation log + universal undo (inspired by Jujutsu)                    [L, biggest differentiator]
     Record every state-changing rgit command (refs before/after, index hash, HEAD) in .git/rgit-oplog/. Then:
        rgit oplog            list operations ("12:03 commit -m 'x'", "12:07 reset --hard HEAD~1")
        rgit undo / redo      move refs back/forward one operation
        rgit undo --to <id>   time-travel; `rgit oplog show <id>` shows what changed
     Even `reset --hard` becomes safe if working-tree snapshots are stored as dangling-but-referenced tree objects.
     This single feature is what makes the "verbose & friendly" promise credible: nothing is scary any more.

C.7  Guided mode / interactive helpers                                      [M]
     rgit tutor           In-terminal lessons that run in test-sandbox-style temp repos (init → commit → branch → merge conflict...).
     rgit guide           "What do you want to do?" menu: undo last commit / discard changes / share my work / fix a conflict ...
                          → maps to the right commands and runs them with explanations.
     Confirmation prompts for destructive actions in interactive terminals (skip with --yes or when not a TTY).
     `rgit hint off` / rgit.hints = false so power users aren't nagged.

C.8  Presentation polish                                                    [S-M]
     - Colour with meaning (green = safe, yellow = changes history, red = destroys data); respect NO_COLOR and non-TTY.
     - Emoji-free by default, `rgit.icons = true` for ✔ ⚠ ✖ symbols.
     - Progress + summary lines for long operations (clone, fetch, gc, repack) in normal mode.
     - Relative dates + human sizes ("3 minutes ago", "1.2 MiB") in explain mode; ISO stays in quiet mode.
     - `--json` output for status / log / branch / oplog / doctor (editor & tooling integration; also makes testing easier).
     - Shell completions (`rgit completions bash|zsh|fish|powershell`) — clap gives this almost for free via clap_complete. [S]
     - Aliases in plain language: `rgit unstage <f>` (= restore --staged), `rgit discard <f>` (= restore, with warning),
       `rgit last` (= show HEAD), `rgit amend`, `rgit uncommit`, `rgit branches`, `rgit tags`, `rgit current`. Printed alongside the
       git-equivalent in explain mode, so users learn both vocabularies.

C.9  Learning-friendly plumbing output                                      [S]
     `rgit cat-file --explain <hash>`: describes an object ("commit by Sebi on 2026-11-02, tree 4f2a…, 1 parent, message: …").
     `rgit inspect .git`: annotated tour of the .git directory (HEAD, refs/, objects/, index, logs/) — fits rgit's origin as a
     learning project and is great for the README / thesis demo.
     `rgit --trace`: print every object read/written and ref updated by a command (teach level, but for debugging).


D. SUGGESTED ORDER
------------------
 1. src/ui.rs + verbosity levels (C.1) and the shared 3-part error type (C.2)      <- foundation, touches every command lightly
 2. status / reset / checkout / diff headers (C.3)                                 <- the 4 most-confusing commands, quick visible win
 3. whereami, oops, doctor, ignore, branch-clean                                  <- cheap new commands that feel "rgit"
 4. operation log + undo/redo (C.6)                                               <- the big differentiator
 5. rename detection (log --graph/--follow, patch mode done)                                        <- biggest fidelity gaps
 6. explain / why / glossary (C.4, C.5), completions, --json                      <- polish and teaching layer
 7. index conflict stages, rebase --onto/--autosquash, merge flags                <- deeper git parity
 8. story / hotspots / stats / changelog                                          <- "insight" commands for the demo

E. GUARD-RAILS SO "FRIENDLY" DOESN'T BREAK "CORRECT"
----------------------------------------------------
 - Prose goes to stderr or behind verbosity >= explain; stdout in quiet mode stays byte-identical to git where it is today
   (your tests against real git depend on it).
 - Never change what a command DOES for friendliness — only what it says (the exception is opt-in safety nets: auto-snapshot, prompts).
 - Keep messages in one place (ui.rs / a messages table) so they can be translated later (Romanian + English fits your audience).
 - Add a tiny golden-output test suite (rtest-driven): each ambiguous command × each verbosity level, so wording changes are intentional.
